<h2>The challenge</h2>

<p>The Armed Forces Medical Services tendered for thirteen RO water plants for hemodialysis units — equipment where water purity is directly a matter of patient safety. The tender required the original equipment manufacturer to have a minimum turnover of ₹33 crores. The petitioner, a micro enterprise with a turnover of about ₹75 lakhs, argued the condition was self-contradictory and arbitrary: the same tender extended purchase preference to MSEs under the Public Procurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises Order, 2012, yet fixed an entry bar that no micro enterprise — statutorily capped at ₹10 crores turnover — could ever cross.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>

<p>The Bench dismissed the petition on three grounds. First, the policy framework itself contemplates exceptions: the Office Memorandum of 20 September 2016 permits procuring entities, for reasons recorded, to decline relaxation of prior-turnover and prior-experience criteria — and procurements bearing on public health and safety are the paradigm case. Dialysis-grade water plants sit squarely within that category, and sustained financial capability and after-sales support were legitimate concerns for capital medical equipment. Second, eligibility and preference operate sequentially: purchase preference is not applicable merely because the bidder is a micro enterprise; it assumes an otherwise eligible bid. Third, judicial review of tender terms remains restrained — the author of the tender is the best judge of its requirements, absent mala fides or perversity.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The Bench did not leave the anomaly unremarked. A tender that promises MSE preference while fixing an MSE-impossible threshold contains a real inconsistency, and the Court urged correction in future tenders. The observation matters: it acknowledges that preference clauses cannot be reduced to boilerplate, even where a particular procurement lawfully declines relaxation.</p>
</div>

<h2>The framework at a glance</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Instrument</th><th>What it provides</th></tr>
  <tr><td>MSMED Act, 2006 &amp; classification norms</td><td>Defines micro, small and medium enterprises by investment and turnover; micro enterprises capped at ₹10 crore turnover.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Public Procurement Policy for MSEs Order, 2012</td><td>25% annual procurement target from MSEs for central ministries, departments and PSUs; price-matching purchase preference for eligible MSEs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>OM dated 20.09.2016</td><td>Relaxation of prior turnover and experience for MSEs — but procuring entities may decline relaxation, for recorded reasons, where quality or public health and safety so require.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Judicial review standard</td><td>Tender terms interfered with only for arbitrariness, mala fides or perversity — not because a different criterion was possible.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical significance</h2>

<p>For MSEs bidding on government tenders, the decision clarifies where the leverage lies. The relaxation regime is the rule, and its denial is the exception requiring justification — so in procurements without a genuine health or safety character, an MSE confronted with an exclusionary turnover or experience bar has a substantial ground of challenge, mounted promptly at the tender stage. For procuring authorities, the judgment is equally a compliance note: record the justification for declining relaxation, and align preference clauses with eligibility thresholds so tenders do not promise with one clause what they withdraw with another.</p>

